<Layout
  title="Rubin - Hardware - Rycode"
  description="The Rubin GPU is 50 PFLOPS of NVFP4 inference and 288 GB of HBM4. Vera Rubin NVL72 is 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs."
  variant="ai"
>
  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Hardware · Chips</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">Rubin</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">Rubin is the NVIDIA GPU family that follows <a href="/ai/hardware/chips/blackwell/">Blackwell</a>. One Rubin GPU delivers 50 PFLOPS of NVFP4 inference and carries 288 GB of HBM4. The Vera Rubin NVL72 is the rack built around it, with 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs. Meta's purchase is on the <a href="/ai/hardware/chips/meta/">Meta stack</a>.</p>
    <ChipFigure src="/ai/hardware/chips/rubin.jpg" alt="NVIDIA Rubin GPU package render: the compute silicon in the center with four memory stacks above and four below, inside a silver frame on a black background." caption="NVIDIA Rubin GPU." credit="Image: NVIDIA" />
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Rubin GPU</h2>
    <p>NVIDIA announced Rubin on 5 January 2026. The Rubin GPU delivers 50 petaflops of NVFP4 compute for AI inference, which is running a trained model to answer requests. NVFP4 is NVIDIA's 4-bit number format, and a petaflop is a thousand trillion math operations per second. Compared with the Blackwell platform, NVIDIA says Rubin cuts the cost of each inference token by up to 10 times, where a token is a small chunk of text. It also says Rubin needs 4 times fewer GPUs to train mixture-of-experts models, which split the work among many smaller expert networks inside one model. These are NVIDIA's own claims. NVIDIA says Rubin is in full production and that partners will offer Rubin-based products in the second half of 2026.</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table ai-model-table--prose">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Figure</th>
            <th scope="col">Rubin GPU</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>NVFP4 inference</td>
            <td>50 PFLOPS, sparse</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>NVFP4 training</td>
            <td>35 PFLOPS, dense</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FP8/FP6 training</td>
            <td>17.5 PFLOPS, dense</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FP16/BF16</td>
            <td>4 PFLOPS, dense</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>TF32</td>
            <td>2 PFLOPS, dense</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FP32</td>
            <td>130 TFLOPS</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FP64</td>
            <td>33 TFLOPS</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Memory</td>
            <td>288 GB HBM4 at 19.2 TB/s</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>NVLink (GPU-to-GPU link)</td>
            <td>3 TB/s</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Networking</td>
            <td>0.45 TB/s</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Training is teaching a model. Sparse figures count a shortcut that skips zeros in the math, so they read higher, and dense figures count every number. FP8 stores each number in 8 bits and FP6 in 6 bits. FP16, BF16, TF32, FP32, and FP64 are larger number formats that are more precise but slower. HBM4 is the fourth generation of high-bandwidth memory, the fast memory stacked next to the chip. NVLink is NVIDIA's wiring that lets GPUs share work.</p>

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Vera Rubin NVL72</h2>
    <ChipFigure src="/ai/hardware/chips/rubin-rack.jpg" alt="NVIDIA rendering of one Vera Rubin NVL72 rack at a three-quarter angle on a black background: an enclosed dark blue cabinet with a lit column of gold compute trays behind its front opening." caption="NVIDIA rendering of the Vera Rubin NVL72 rack." credit="Image: NVIDIA" variant="rack" />
    <p>The 5 January 2026 release says the Vera Rubin NVL72 combines 72 Rubin GPUs, 36 Vera CPUs, and NVLink 6, the sixth generation of NVIDIA's chip-to-chip wiring. It is a rack-scale system, meaning the whole server rack is built as one machine.</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table ai-model-table--prose">
